    "evidence_text": "Recent work shows that unsupervised singing voice conversion can be achieved with an autoencoder-based approach [1].\n\nHowever, the converted singing voice can be easily out of key, showing that the existing approach can not model the pitch information precisely.\n\nWe also demonstrate that the pitch of converted singing can be easily controlled during generation by changing the levels of extracted pitch before passing it to the decoder network.\n\non.\n\nTraditional singing synthesize systems are based on concatenative  or HMM  based approaches.\n\nFor example,  introduces a singing synthesis method using an architecture similar to WaveNet .\n\nIt adopts lyrics and notes as input and generates vocoder features autoregressively for final singing voice synthesis.\n\nSinging voice conversion is another way of singing synthesis which extracts musical expression within existing singing and reproduced them with another singer's voice.\n\nThere are several singing voice conversion methods to convert one's singing voice to another , , .\n\nThey generally require parallel data to train the conversion model.\n\nA confusion loss  is also introduced to force the encoder to learn a singer-invariant representation.\n\nBy switching between embeddings of different singers during generation, the singing voice conversion can be achieved.\n\nGo back to step 1.\n\nFurthermore, backtranslation and mixup techniques  are also used to improve the quality of the converted singing voice.\n\na kernel size of 3 and channels of 100.\n\nAudio samples are available at our website .\n\nEvery singer provided 4 songs.\n\nDuring testing, We converted each one's singing voice to the other five singer's voice.\n\nBefore training, We converted the songs to monophonic audio of\n\n16kHz sample rate and PCM-16 bit format.\n\nBefore feeding them into the model, we normalized the value of pitch between 0 and 1.\n\nThe dropout probability in the singer classification network and pitch regression network were both $0.2$.\n\nDuring the training process, backtranslation\n\nand mixup  were employed to improve the conversion.\n\nThe automatic score roughly followed the design in .\n\nThe higher the score is, the better the output pitch matches the input pitch.\n\nPitchNet.\n\nThe evaluated automatic scores on conversion and reconstruction tasks are shown in Tab.\n\nTABREF14.\n\nOur method performed better both on conversion and reconstruction.\n\nTwo questions were asked: (1) what is quality of the audio?\n\n(naturalness) (2) How well does the converted version match the original?\n\nBesides, the converted samples provided by  was also included to give a more convincing evaluation.\n\nAs shown by Tab.\n\nNext we qualitatively analyze the influence of input pitch in our method.\n\nWe used different pitch as input to observe how the output pitch\n\nwould change along with the input pitch.\n\nAnd the output pitch was also extracted by the pitch tracker of the librosa package.\n\nFig. FIGREF16 plots the pitch of input audio and output audio with different pitch as input while keeping the target singer the same.\n\nAs shown by Fig. FIGREF16, the output pitch changes significantly along with the input pitch.\n\nConclusion\tIn this paper, a novel unsupervised singing voice conversion method named PitchNet is proposed.\n\nOur method outperforms the existing unsupervised singing voice conversion method and achieves flexible pitch manipulation.",
